1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:05,120
I think a gigantic opportunity has just opened up and we need to talk about it.

2
00:00:05,422 --> 00:00:17,316
Meta has opened up Muse, its personal AI agent to developers, and you can now submit something called a connector, which gives Muse a way to use your service when someone asks for help.

3
00:00:17,646 --> 00:00:20,880
And I think this could be the app store moment for AI.

4
00:00:21,134 --> 00:00:30,560
So if you're thinking about starting a business or you already have one, it's worth understanding what just became possible because billions of dollars are going to change hands.

5
00:00:30,862 --> 00:00:37,360
By the end of this video, you'll understand what Muse connectors are and where I'd look for business opportunities.

6
00:00:37,646 --> 00:00:42,800
We're gonna walk through how you can build one using a coding agent and submit it to Meta.

7
00:00:42,960 --> 00:00:47,588
We'll also spend some time on how people actually find what you've built.

8
00:00:47,886 --> 00:00:52,880
Because let's be real, that's a big part of whether any of this turns into a business.

9
00:00:53,262 --> 00:00:57,760
I'll give you four Muse Connector startup ideas for free by the end of this video.

10
00:00:58,062 --> 00:01:05,520
And if you're new here, my name is Greg Eisenberg, and I'm the host of the Startup Ideas Podcast, and I know a thing or two about social apps.

11
00:01:05,680 --> 00:01:11,748
In the past life, I was an advisor to TikTok, an advisor to Reddit, and now I build AI native businesses.

12
00:01:12,238 --> 00:01:20,320
If this episode gets 7,000 likes, comments, and subscribes, I'll drop a Muse Connector guide 100% free with more ideas.

13
00:01:20,462 --> 00:01:24,160
I'll include the link in the pinned comment description if that happens.

14
00:01:24,462 --> 00:01:25,520
So go ahead and do that.

15
00:01:25,840 --> 00:01:27,680
All right, let's start the episode.

16
00:01:35,566 --> 00:01:40,548
Think about what happened when Apple opened the App Store in 2008.

17
00:01:41,134 --> 00:01:42,660
Apple had built the phone.

18
00:01:43,310 --> 00:01:50,020
Now an outside developer could build something useful for the people who owned it and sell it through the store.

19
00:01:50,510 --> 00:01:53,444
Someone can build a photo editing app and charge for it.

20
00:01:54,190 --> 00:02:02,320
Apple supplied a way to distribute the software and collect payment so that the developer could focus on making something people wanted to use.

21
00:02:02,414 --> 00:02:04,292
And it was a win-win scenario, by the way.

22
00:02:04,590 --> 00:02:07,920
Apple got a way better app store and developers made money.

23
00:02:08,080 --> 00:02:18,084
By June 2010, less than two years after the store had opened, Apple had paid developers a billion dollars from paid apps and in-app purchases.

24
00:02:18,542 --> 00:02:24,900
So this new market had formed around the phone, and outside businesses were earning a bunch of money from it.

25
00:02:25,230 --> 00:02:28,708
That's why I'm interested in this Muse announcement.

26
00:02:29,040 --> 00:02:35,940
Meta is building the assistant, and other businesses could supply services that help it complete a customer's request.

27
00:02:36,558 --> 00:02:42,180
How big that market will become will depend on whether people actually use Muse for those jobs.

28
00:02:42,574 --> 00:02:44,720
But the opening for developers is now.

29
00:02:44,880 --> 00:02:50,884
And yes, I know someone in the comments say comment section is saying, Well, who knows if this is going to be a big app?

30
00:02:51,374 --> 00:02:56,644
No one really knows, but it is today, as of recording, the number one app in the app store.

31
00:02:57,422 --> 00:03:05,540
When I'm thinking about the app store analogy versus the app store for AI analogy, there's another interesting wrinkle.

32
00:03:05,902 --> 00:03:09,920
So your service can become useful halfway through a bigger request.

33
00:03:10,094 --> 00:03:11,396
Let me explain what I mean by that.

34
00:03:11,982 --> 00:03:20,772
Take someone who's organizing a small event, they're going to need a venue, and later they realize they need maybe some equipment that they need to rent.

35
00:03:21,200 --> 00:03:30,372
A company that handles equipment rentals could become relevant at that moment, even though renting equipment wasn't what started the conversation.

36
00:03:30,766 --> 00:03:35,492
As a founder, as someone who's trying to build something, I would closely look at those moments.

37
00:03:36,014 --> 00:03:44,484
Someone is trying to accomplish something, and there's a step where they need access to a business that can actually deliver and money's changing hands.

38
00:03:45,038 --> 00:03:50,820
Meta's platform now has a submission process and a directory of approved connectors.

39
00:03:51,022 --> 00:03:55,364
There's a bunch of big companies in there now, and I think there's gonna be a bunch of small and medium sized soon.

40
00:03:56,046 --> 00:03:57,796
Editors also can feature them.

41
00:03:58,190 --> 00:04:00,480
That gives something real to explore.

42
00:04:00,750 --> 00:04:03,760
I remember when I was launching apps in the early days.

43
00:04:04,654 --> 00:04:10,752
And I got featured and I was getting like 40,000 downloads every single day from that feature.

44
00:04:10,832 --> 00:04:11,780
It was all free.

45
00:04:12,142 --> 00:04:18,992
With Muse AI connectors, what we still need to learn is how people will find those services and how often they use them.

46
00:04:19,278 --> 00:04:24,324
But I wouldn't be surprised if the directory generated a bunch of customers.

47
00:04:24,622 --> 00:04:29,872
That's why being early could help you learn faster than other people in your category.

48
00:04:30,032 --> 00:04:36,512
So I would use that time to understand a customer's request and make my service work really well for it.

49
00:04:36,814 --> 00:04:38,756
And then who knows, you might get featured.

50
00:04:39,152 --> 00:04:54,916
But we're also towards the end of the episode are going to talk about a bunch of other techniques to get downloads or not really downloads to get usage to your connector that isn't reliant on meta featuring you.

51
00:04:55,566 --> 00:04:58,148
So, how does a Muse connector actually work?

52
00:04:58,512 --> 00:05:05,872
Say I tell Muse I need a podcast studio on a Tuesday afternoon for two hours, and my budget is $200.

53
00:05:06,830 --> 00:05:11,684
It needs to find out which city I'm in and what kind of recording I'm gonna be doing.

54
00:05:12,192 --> 00:05:17,604
Then it needs a business that can answer whether a suitable room is available.

55
00:05:17,872 --> 00:05:26,116
An API is a way for one piece of software to ask another piece of software for something, it's just at the core of it.

56
00:05:26,542 --> 00:05:30,032
You can think of it as a service window at the studio.

57
00:05:30,286 --> 00:05:37,392
You make a specific request, like checking Tuesday's availability, and you get the specific answer back.

58
00:05:37,902 --> 00:05:41,792
The connector lets Muse use that window.

59
00:05:42,158 --> 00:05:49,860
So the studio system might return an available room at, I don't know, $160, including the equipment I need.

60
00:05:50,446 --> 00:05:57,252
Muse can show me the offer, and once I approve it, the service can make the booking and then it sends back a confirmation.

61
00:05:57,806 --> 00:06:03,716
That's a proposed workflow, but it shows what the connection is there to accomplish.

62
00:06:04,110 --> 00:06:07,872
The studio still has to keep its calendar accurate, right?

63
00:06:08,174 --> 00:06:11,172
Someone has to make sure the equipment works, of course.

64
00:06:11,566 --> 00:06:17,540
If a customer needs help getting into the building, the business needs a way to handle that too.

65
00:06:18,094 --> 00:06:21,952
What's cool is an agent makes it easier to ask for the service.

66
00:06:22,190 --> 00:06:24,512
The service still has to be good, though.

67
00:06:24,782 --> 00:06:27,268
You can see a real version of this in travel.

68
00:06:27,886 --> 00:06:35,524
So there's a company called Duffel, and they say their Muse integration lets people search flights and manage bookings.

69
00:06:36,130 --> 00:06:43,972
Duffel also said that its business has passed a billion dollars in annual transaction value on their, I guess, web app and mobile app.

70
00:06:44,192 --> 00:06:47,748
So now they're betting on the Muse AI connector thing.

71
00:06:48,366 --> 00:06:54,432
That's the value of bookings, by the way, passing through its business rather than Duffel's revenue.

72
00:06:54,512 --> 00:06:56,260
And it covers the whole company.

73
00:06:56,622 --> 00:07:05,508
But it's a useful example of a company that already provides something valuable becoming accessible through a new interface.

74
00:07:05,902 --> 00:07:12,260
If you built software before, I'd start by looking at what your customers can already do through your API.

75
00:07:12,526 --> 00:07:16,644
There might be a useful connector sitting inside the product that you already have.

76
00:07:17,326 --> 00:07:24,484
So if I wanted to start a Muse connector business, where would I actually start a business?

77
00:07:25,518 --> 00:07:29,444
Here are four ideas I want to walk you through that I think are pretty good.

78
00:07:29,870 --> 00:07:33,632
The first idea is just doing lead gen for business suppliers.

79
00:07:33,902 --> 00:07:35,232
Let me explain what I mean by that.

80
00:07:35,566 --> 00:07:37,700
Say you run a linen service in Miami.

81
00:07:37,998 --> 00:07:41,028
You supply restaurants with clean tablecloths and napkins.

82
00:07:41,582 --> 00:07:46,436
You could ask Muse which restaurants are opening nearby that might need our service.

83
00:07:47,150 --> 00:07:51,556
And your connector reaches a service you've built to track those openings.

84
00:07:52,782 --> 00:08:02,404
It brings back relevant restaurants with a source showing when each is expected to open and a public business contact when it's available, of course.

85
00:08:02,894 --> 00:08:06,948
The customer can see why each restaurant might be worth approaching.

86
00:08:07,374 --> 00:08:10,272
I'd start with one city and maybe just one supplier.

87
00:08:10,606 --> 00:08:19,876
You can use sources you're allowed to access, by the way, like local business announcements, and then you verify the details before adding a restaurant.

88
00:08:20,334 --> 00:08:22,500
A planned opening might get delayed.

89
00:08:22,926 --> 00:08:26,788
Someone might announce a second location when you're still showing the first.

90
00:08:27,118 --> 00:08:31,556
So keeping that information current is going to be a big part of what you're selling here.

91
00:08:32,046 --> 00:08:34,112
General contact search already exists.

92
00:08:34,192 --> 00:08:42,512
I mean, I'm sure you've seen things like Apollo, but your opportunity is knowing something timely about a specific market.

93
00:08:43,054 --> 00:08:50,404
So a restaurant preparing to open gives the linen company like a concrete reason to start a conversation.

94
00:08:50,798 --> 00:08:54,308
Although you still have to find out whether it needs a supplier, of course.

95
00:08:54,894 --> 00:09:01,632
I'd show a linen company a few verified opportunities, and I'd ask whether it would pay for fresh ones.

96
00:09:02,382 --> 00:09:04,388
Every week, every two weeks, every three weeks.

97
00:09:04,846 --> 00:09:06,500
And then you can charge a subscription.

98
00:09:07,246 --> 00:09:18,032
Just a hypothetical example here, but like a hundred customers paying $99 a month would give you $9,900 in MRR before any of your costs.

99
00:09:18,192 --> 00:09:23,072
So you can create a nice little business here doing lead gen for business suppliers.

100
00:09:23,342 --> 00:09:26,308
And I chose local just as an example because I think it's niche.

101
00:09:26,542 --> 00:09:28,132
So that's startup idea number one.

102
00:09:28,686 --> 00:09:32,292
Startup idea number two is home repair dispatch.

103
00:09:32,974 --> 00:09:36,672
So they tell Muse, I don't know, my dishwasher is broken.

104
00:09:36,832 --> 00:09:42,916
Find someone who repairs this exact, you know, Samsung model and can come tomorrow.

105
00:09:43,630 --> 00:09:53,552
So your service is going to go check with participating repair companies and it returns the ones that handle that specific appliance.

106
00:09:53,632 --> 00:09:58,672
So Samsung, this model, and that obviously they have confirmed availability.

107
00:09:58,752 --> 00:10:00,612
They can actually go and fix the thing.

108
00:10:01,070 --> 00:10:13,252
The customer then chooses a provider and agrees to share the job details, and your service passes the request along, or it could book the appointment where that's supported.

109
00:10:13,582 --> 00:10:22,500
So you could charge the repair company like an agreed upon fee, $100, let's say, for a qualified intro or a confirmed booking.

110
00:10:23,150 --> 00:10:29,860
Thumbtack, big company, they're already validated that people will pay for customer leads.

111
00:10:30,222 --> 00:10:35,172
So your service would have to deliver requests those providers actually want.

112
00:10:35,598 --> 00:10:39,844
I'd begin with, one type of repair, one city, one town.

113
00:10:40,174 --> 00:10:43,236
You get to know the providers and what they can actually handle.

114
00:10:43,630 --> 00:10:50,468
A confirm opening tomorrow is useful information when someone is standing in a kitchen with a broken appliance.

115
00:10:51,182 --> 00:10:54,500
That homeowner might only need you occasionally.

116
00:10:54,958 --> 00:10:57,312
The repair company needs that steady flow, though.

117
00:10:57,392 --> 00:10:59,108
They need customers every single day.

118
00:10:59,374 --> 00:11:06,948
And that's why I'd look at demand across the local market as well as how often one person actually comes back.

119
00:11:07,566 --> 00:11:09,632
Startup idea number three is a fun one.

120
00:11:09,806 --> 00:11:13,284
You know, if you know me, I like racket sports.

121
00:11:14,032 --> 00:11:18,032
And, you know, where I live, paddle is getting really, really popular.

122
00:11:18,446 --> 00:11:23,684
So startup idea number three is a local paddle match and court finder.

123
00:11:24,430 --> 00:11:32,900
So someone opens up Muse and they ask, hey, find me a paddle game tonight near me with people around my level.

124
00:11:33,358 --> 00:11:43,684
So your service is gonna go look in look for open spots at participating clubs and and courts, and it's gonna show suitable games and how much it's going to cost.

125
00:11:44,206 --> 00:11:46,912
The player is gonna approve the reservation.

126
00:11:47,214 --> 00:11:53,444
If they already have a group, they could ask for a court instead and share the booking details with their friends.

127
00:11:53,870 --> 00:12:00,192
I'd imagine, by the way, that Muse is gonna get more and more social over the next six to 12 months.

128
00:12:00,352 --> 00:12:01,872
I mean, we're talking about meta, right?

129
00:12:01,952 --> 00:12:03,556
They understand social better than anyone.

130
00:12:03,950 --> 00:12:05,668
I would start with One City.

131
00:12:06,030 --> 00:12:08,636
You know, I think it's called Playtomic.

132
00:12:08,636 --> 00:12:11,152
they offer court and match booking.

133
00:12:11,374 --> 00:12:18,372
So I'd look for a useful gap, such as local clubs whose availability players currently have to check separately.

134
00:12:18,702 --> 00:12:26,192
You need those clubs, by the way, to obviously let you access their opening and make reservations.

135
00:12:26,638 --> 00:12:28,004
some do and some won't.

136
00:12:28,462 --> 00:12:32,388
But you can earn an agreed fee on these bookings.

137
00:12:33,952 --> 00:12:39,620
a club with an empty slot has something they they want to sell, right?

138
00:12:40,206 --> 00:12:47,232
And someone who plays several times a week has a reason to keep using a service that helps them get a game.

139
00:12:47,694 --> 00:12:55,204
Startup idea number four is a family dinner planning and grocery integration connector.

140
00:12:55,886 --> 00:13:03,524
So a parent might say, Hey, sort out dinners for the next three nights, 20 minutes of cooking each night max.

141
00:13:04,272 --> 00:13:06,564
we already have rice and broccoli.

142
00:13:07,214 --> 00:13:14,032
So your service is gonna build the plan from recipes you've tested for that specific kind of family.

143
00:13:14,318 --> 00:13:19,236
It adjusts the quantities, it turns the missing ingredients into a shopping list that they can go buy from.

144
00:13:19,758 --> 00:13:28,964
Instacart, their developer tools actually let you create a shoppable list that the customer opens to review products and finish checkout.

145
00:13:29,294 --> 00:13:34,432
So that gives you like a practical starting point for helping someone get the ingredients home.

146
00:13:34,734 --> 00:13:38,832
By the way, side note, maybe someone like Instacart ends up just acquiring this.

147
00:13:39,086 --> 00:13:46,832
Like there might be a bunch of these connectors that get acquired you know, over the next couple of years if this gets big.

148
00:13:47,790 --> 00:13:54,976
If I if I was trying to test this business, you know, I test a subscription with families who want to use it every week, right?

149
00:13:55,086 --> 00:13:56,644
The meals have to work for them.

150
00:13:57,230 --> 00:14:10,052
But if their kids like the dinners and the portions make sense and they have a reason to use your service for the next grocery shop, you know, I'd want to learn what they actually cooked and use that feedback to improve the next plan.

151
00:14:10,510 --> 00:14:17,092
You know, of these four ideas, I'd probably investigate the lead generation idea first if I was starting with a small budget.

152
00:14:17,696 --> 00:14:24,016
you can put together a useful sample and show it to a potential customer before writing much software.

153
00:14:24,096 --> 00:14:26,084
And that's what I like about those types of businesses.

154
00:14:26,574 --> 00:14:33,828
For someone who already knows a bunch of local service providers, the home repair idea might be a closer look.

155
00:14:34,126 --> 00:14:40,576
But yeah, I tried to give you a few different types of startup ideas, more consumer, more business-y.

156
00:14:40,976 --> 00:14:45,536
My guess is the consumer ideas are the most fun to do.

157
00:14:45,776 --> 00:14:50,116
And because it's meta, they're more of a consumer app company, there's more of those use cases.

158
00:14:50,816 --> 00:14:56,256
But I think there's going to be a lot of money and also business use cases as well.

159
00:14:56,656 --> 00:15:03,556
Of course, this brings us to the question I'd have if I was watching or listening to this, which is great.

160
00:15:03,936 --> 00:15:05,668
I have a connector.

161
00:15:05,936 --> 00:15:08,736
How are people going to learn about my connector?

162
00:15:08,816 --> 00:15:10,724
How are they going to learn about my business?

163
00:15:11,854 --> 00:15:16,964
Obviously, getting into the directory gets a lot of exposure.

164
00:15:18,318 --> 00:15:27,108
but whether an unknown service is going to get recommended, during a general conversation is something we still need evidence for.

165
00:15:27,470 --> 00:15:32,292
So I'd build my initial plan around customers I have a way to reach.

166
00:15:33,230 --> 00:15:38,212
So the first idea I have for you is work with existing creators who have audiences.

167
00:15:38,606 --> 00:15:44,324
So for the family dinner service, that could be a creator who shares vegetarian recipes for parents.

168
00:15:44,846 --> 00:15:49,956
And then you help them demonstrate how to turn a few meals into a grocery order.

169
00:15:50,286 --> 00:15:58,244
They give their audience the tested setup instructions, and you agree on how they'll be paid for customers they introduce.

170
00:15:58,702 --> 00:16:04,228
The viewer has seen a task they want to accomplish and understands why they connect the service.

171
00:16:04,590 --> 00:16:10,756
I start with one relevant creator, and I see how many people complete that first task.

172
00:16:11,118 --> 00:16:17,540
The size of the audience matters less to me, honestly, than whether the people in it need what you're selling.

173
00:16:17,838 --> 00:16:30,176
The second idea I have for you is for the paddle service, for example, one person books a court and shares a page with other three players showing the time and location.

174
00:16:30,336 --> 00:16:34,564
So this idea is basically product-driven viral sharing.

175
00:16:34,990 --> 00:16:36,932
Some people call it product-led growth too.

176
00:16:37,518 --> 00:16:41,764
But basically, it's how do you get one person to share your product?

177
00:16:42,336 --> 00:16:48,576
in the paddle service idea, those players get something useful from your product before they ever sign up.

178
00:16:48,942 --> 00:16:55,204
So if you give them an easy way to arrange the next game, you know, you have a possible route to another booking.

179
00:16:55,694 --> 00:16:59,216
So you can choose ideas with this kind of sharing built into the work.

180
00:16:59,296 --> 00:17:06,656
You can kind of prioritize based on these multiplayer ideas because that's going to help you, you know, drive a lot of growth.

181
00:17:06,736 --> 00:17:14,736
And we saw that, you know, in the mobile era, you know, a lot of social apps, a lot of apps had you know social features to it.

182
00:17:14,958 --> 00:17:17,028
And those are the ones that became really popular.

183
00:17:17,966 --> 00:17:21,136
Same is true uh in this agentic era.

184
00:17:21,840 --> 00:17:23,680
So, you know, what's an example of that?

185
00:17:23,854 --> 00:17:28,960
You know, a proposal gets sent to someone, an event invitation gets passed around.

186
00:17:29,358 --> 00:17:32,804
You know, make the result useful to the person receiving it.

187
00:17:33,070 --> 00:17:36,960
And then some of those people may become customers themselves.

188
00:17:37,040 --> 00:17:37,636
That's the hope.

189
00:17:38,080 --> 00:17:46,628
So I'd measure that before I'd call it a growth loop, but it's a practical thing to look for when you're choosing between, you know, two or three promising ideas.

190
00:17:47,118 --> 00:17:52,708
The third idea I have for you is distribution through existing connected marketplaces.

191
00:17:53,166 --> 00:18:04,132
So Ticketmaster says eligible events can now appear through its connector without each organizer doing additional integration work.

192
00:18:04,462 --> 00:18:13,028
So an organizer now could reach someone through the Ticketmaster experience while continuing to run their own event business.

193
00:18:13,390 --> 00:18:18,660
That makes me curious about where each connected service gets its supply.

194
00:18:19,040 --> 00:18:32,400
If you're in an industry with an existing marketplace, investigate how businesses participate and what information helps customers choose them accurately.

195
00:18:32,560 --> 00:18:36,880
So for some people, that could be the most practical way into this market.

196
00:18:37,440 --> 00:18:44,196
for others, building their own connector makes sense because they already have customers or a partner who can introduce them.

197
00:18:44,686 --> 00:18:46,560
So I'd make that decision early.

198
00:18:46,926 --> 00:18:58,724
You want to be able to point to a specific person or channel that can bring your first customers, even while you're hoping the directory becomes a meaningful source of demand.

199
00:18:59,758 --> 00:19:05,828
Of course, Route Four is just getting feature placement, in meta's directory.

200
00:19:06,240 --> 00:19:09,440
But let's be real, you're not gonna be able to bank on that.

201
00:19:09,920 --> 00:19:16,800
when I had that app store feature, that first app store feature, it was just like basically somewhat random.

202
00:19:16,960 --> 00:19:22,400
I woke up and all of a sudden I was like, why is why is the chart vertically?

203
00:19:22,480 --> 00:19:26,084
And I think a lot of people are gonna have that experience with the meta directory.

